{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 14, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a significant figure in the Crusades and a powerful ruler in the Levant navigating complex political landscapes and enduring numerous conflicts. His reign saw both territorial expansion and internal struggles shaping the future of Tripoli.\n\n*Early Life and Accession*\n\nRaymond was born around 1120 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Ermengarde of Hauteville. He received extensive military training preparing him for leadership. Upon his father's death in 1154 Raymond inherited the County of Tripoli at a young age facing immediate challenges to his authority. These challenges included rival claimants internal dissent and external threats from Muslim forces. He quickly asserted his power suppressing rebellions and consolidating his control over the county. His early years were marked by a determined effort to secure Tripoli's position as a vital Crusader stronghold.\n\nRaymond III’s reign was defined by constant warfare. He fought against the Zengids a powerful Muslim dynasty that controlled much of Syria. These conflicts involved sieges battles and shifting alliances. He also dealt with internal power struggles involving powerful families within Tripoli. His military campaigns were often costly but ultimately helped to maintain Tripoli’s independence and protect its trade routes. He relied on mercenaries and allied with other Crusader states such as Antioch and Jerusalem to bolster his forces.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances*\n\nRaymond was not solely a warrior he was also a skilled diplomat. He understood the importance of alliances in maintaining his power. He forged relationships with various rulers including the Holy Roman Emperor Frederick II. These alliances provided crucial support against common enemies and helped to secure Tripoli’s strategic position. He engaged in complex negotiations seeking to balance his relationships with both Christian and Muslim powers. His diplomatic efforts were essential in navigating the turbulent political environment of the Levant. He often used marriage alliances to strengthen his position and secure truces.\n\n*Legacy and Death*\n\nRaymond III died in 1199 leaving the County of Tripoli to his nephew Raymond IV. His reign is remembered as a period of resilience and strategic maneuvering. He successfully defended Tripoli against numerous threats and maintained its importance as a Crusader state for several decades. While his rule was marked by constant warfare and internal challenges he left behind a strengthened and more secure county. His efforts to balance military strength with diplomatic skill set a precedent for future rulers of Tripoli. His death marked a turning point in the county's history ushering in a new era of challenges and opportunities. He is considered one of the most important Counts of Tripoli.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.2371193766593933}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.20648594200611115}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.12102461606264114}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.00020811776630580425}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.1457441107486375e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -9.775113539944869e-06}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.480850500636734e-05}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.001829261309467256}, {"token": "▁until", "logprob": -0.03265677019953728}, {"token": "▁his", "logprob": -0.000248043768806383}, {"token": "▁death", "logprob": -1.680836794548668e-05}, {"token": "▁in", "logprob": -4.83978